From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: brennoflavio <contato@brennoflavio.com.br>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 23:26:19 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] webapi: mock Neo Web Audio context and stereo destination
 natively

Expose a Window-only AudioContext/BaseAudioContext and
AudioDestinationNode/AudioNode hierarchy through native Zig bindings. Model
the base MacBook Neo's built-in stereo output with a synthetic 48 kHz
default, two channels, and plausible latency values; never query host audio.
Honor sample-rate and latency options and preserve native descriptors,
receiver checks, illegal constructors, destination identity and context
back-references. This is a partial facade, not DSP, playback, an audio graph,
an AudioListener, or OfflineAudioContext.

Keep the clock frozen while suspended/closed and advance it in synthetic
128-frame quanta while running. Require trusted sticky activation for
resume, order accepted lifecycle operations, return native Promises and
DOMExceptions, and dispatch asynchronous trusted statechange events.
Detach contexts on navigation and disconnected frames; pending tasks root
their owners, rejection storage survives its originating iframe, and
collectible JS wrapper links preserve callbacks across context/destination
GC. Release callback ownership on explicit, once, and AbortSignal removal
using an optional direct-event listener cleanup hook.

The benchmark audio-sample-rate probe reads AudioContext.sampleRate,
destination.maxChannelCount and awaits close. Temporary diagnostics also
exercise API shape, options, autoplay, lifecycle interleaving, event
ownership, callback collection, iframe lifetime and navigation cleanup.
No upstream tests are added and no resource-loading defaults are changed.

References: benchmark/src/probes/signals.ts; Web Audio API interfaces and
lifecycle algorithms; Apple MacBook Neo technical specifications. Generic
prototype/option behavior was checked against Chrome 154 on Linux; the
synthetic Mac timing values are a chosen persona, not physical measurements.
